#[title, center: PROGRESS MATRIX]

#[section, $md:
    This page tracks current implementation and target status for Moth's accepted core language,
    compiler, build-system and backend design. Package APIs and project-builder-specific behaviour
    are tracked separately in @./packages-and-builders (Packages and Builders Progress).

    Accepted deferred surfaces may appear here. Open design questions and outside-scope
    mechanisms do not. Permanent language exclusions live in @../design-scope (Design scope).

    Invalid-form diagnostic coverage may appear inside the row for the intended feature. This
    page is not a full language reference, roadmap, exclusion inventory or test manifest.

    The compiler and memory design references may describe accepted end-state architecture that
    has not fully landed. This matrix determines general compiler support today. For live borrow
    checker implementation and Boracle work, use @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/blob/main/docs/roadmap/plans/production-borrow-checker.md (the production borrow checker tracker)
    rather than maintaining a second status inventory here.

    Exact numeric types are delivered. The integration suite, harness, benchmarks, first-party sources and executable documentation use exact numeric types, and the harness rejects the retired `numeric_profile` key. The delivered cast and external-boundary contracts use one plain `cast` spelling, exact source/target evidence, occurrence-local authored safety catches and precision-aware foreign float validation. The frontend and HTML-JS use exact-width numeric identities, I32/F64 defaults, type-preserving equal-width arithmetic and body-local numeric constraints through known shapes, generic signatures and direct generic constructors. Receiver conflicts report their witnesses in authored order. Handler fallback constraints participate before defaulting, while every authored group receives validation without dead groups contributing to inference. Core Math, Random and Text registrations use exact F64/I32 types, and numeric proofs carry no profile stamp. Build inputs accept every fixed integer and binary-float contract and keep command numbers as literals until the exact contract materialises them; MON exposes only exact-width values; no numeric profile selection remains. HTML-Wasm rejects every user aggregate holding a fixed-width scalar before lowering, and it cannot yet interpolate a runtime Bool into a template.

    JS/HTML delivers most of the accepted implicit-failure and entry [$html:<code>Error!</code>]
    contract: private propagation, expression-wide catches, eligible authored safety catches,
    success-only runtime fragment publication and [$html:<code>moth dev</code>] entry-error
    reporting. Wasm rejects recoverable numeric failure explicitly; a safety catch whose handler
    is pruned after convergence publishes no backend requirement and does not gate Wasm.
    Conservative proof discharges Add, Subtract, Multiply and Negate over fixed-width integer
    domains with full-range containment; other domains are
    excluded from that discharge; the @../numbers/#checked-arithmetic (checked-arithmetic reference) states which operations can fail.

    Typed Phase 4 is complete and squash-merged into main from `expression-refactor`
    source `534aea1a5`, retaining accepted R1, R2.0–R2.4 and P2 test isolation.
    Historical review `1343c64ec` retains its original scope. R2.4 evidence qualifies
    compiler source `6b855aab9`, not the main squash tree. Its matched standard/focused
    comparisons and local `just validate-full` cover 6,105 primary units, 2,784
    integration target results, ten feature lanes and all 14 scaling series.
    Grouped-not dev HIR fits n^0.88 under n^1.25 in that evidence.
    Fresh Windows Canvas/frontend proof remains pending.
    Phase 5 type/folding reductions are active on `expression-refactor`, with
    Phases 6–8 following in order.

    Use this together with `tests/cases/manifest.toml` when changing language features.

    ## How to read and edit this page

    - Keep rows focused on current compiler, core build-system and backend behavior.
    - Put package and project-builder status in @./packages-and-builders (Packages and Builders Progress).
    - Put user-facing explanations and examples in docs pages under `docs/src/docs/**`.
    - Put implementation sequencing, active project plans and proposals that have
      not been accepted as design in `docs/roadmap/roadmap.md`.
    - Put accepted end-state architecture in the relevant compiler, memory or
      language design reference even when the implementation is incomplete.
    - Put compiler architecture and stage ownership in `docs/compiler-design-overview.md`.
    - Put contributor workflow, benchmarking and tooling details in `CONTRIBUTING.md`.
    - Prefer short coverage summaries over long fixture lists unless fixture names identify a fragile surface.
    - Use the status labels from this page's legend. Put nuance such as removed, reserved,
      not Alpha or backend-specific in the notes column instead of inventing new status labels.

    ## Alpha target

    The supported Alpha target is:

    - frontend parsing and diagnostics
    - typed AST construction
    - HIR lowering
    - borrow validation
    - JavaScript backend
    - HTML project builder
    - docs-style static site generation
]

#[section, $md:
    ## Status legend

    [styled_table:
        [header: [: Status] [: Meaning] ]

        [data:
            [: Supported]
            [: Implemented for the Alpha surface and expected to compile, type check, lower and run through the JS / HTML pipeline.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Partial]
            [: A useful subset is implemented, but some sub-surfaces are intentionally deferred, target-gated or still being shaped before Alpha.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Experimental]
            [: Present in the repo, but not part of the Alpha stability promise.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Deferred]
            [: Planned or design-compatible, but not part of the current implemented Alpha surface.]
        ]
    ]

    ## Coverage legend

    [styled_table:
        [header: [: Coverage] [: Meaning] ]

        [data:
            [: Broad]
            [: Multiple focused cases exist, including success and failure coverage.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Targeted]
            [: Dedicated coverage exists, but not across all important edges.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Thin]
            [: Coverage exists indirectly or through only a small number of cases.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: None]
            [: No clear canonical coverage found.]
        ]
    ]
]

#[section, $md:
    ## Core Alpha surface

    [styled_table:
        [header: [: Surface] [: Status] [: Coverage] [: Runtime target] [: Notes] ]

        [data:
            [: Entry-selected module roots and implicit start]
            [: Supported]
            [: Broad entry-selected start, dependency-sort, normal-file top-level rejection, imported-root suppression, fragment-order and API-only artefact-filtering coverage. Targeted synthetic-start Error!, structured entry-failure and dormant dependency-root grouped-delimiter EOF diagnostics with exact source spans.]
            [: Frontend / HTML builder]
            [: Selected normal modules compile dormant compiler-synthesised [$html:<code>start</code>] work with builtin [$html:<code>Error!</code>]. Entry assembly activates it exactly once. Imported roots expose public interfaces without activating root work. API-only roots remain dependency-bindable and emit no HTML page artefact. The generated caller records returned entry failure structurally and reports a fixed notice independently of [$html:<code>Error.code</code>]. The implicit [$html:<code>start</code>] function is build-system-owned and not user-bindable or callable.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Structured diagnostics]
            [: Supported]
            [: Broad dependency, config, project-structure, syntax, type, rule, borrow, backend-feature and warning coverage plus targeted compiler-owned reason and primary/secondary source-remapping assertions. Synthetic discovery failures retain earlier and failed source snapshots under final identities without repeating preparation. Targeted mixed-cause transport, source/frozen ownership, failed-freeze retention and terminal/terse/dev classification coverage.]
            [: Frontend]
            [: User-facing failures use typed [$html:<code>CompilerDiagnostic</code>] payloads with stable codes, optional qualified reason keys and fixture-relative source expectations. Type diagnostics remain [$html:<code>TypeId</code>]-first. The cold infrastructure aggregate in [$html:<code>CompilerMessages</code>] preserves each [$html:<code>CompilerError</code>]'s type, message, host path, span and metadata with explicit source/frozen owner ranges. Render finalisation preserves the original report and appends any freeze failure. Terminal, five-field terse [$html:<code>MOTH-INFRA-0001</code>] and dev renderers preserve one infrastructure event and distinguish operational failures from compiler defects, requesting bug reports only for defects. The final panic and host-isolation migration remains undelivered.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Variables, assignment and lexical scope]
            [: Partial]
            [: Broad declaration, reassignment, mutability, structured-control-flow scope, shadowing, undefined-mutation and diagnostic coverage. Targeted new-binding collision cases cover parameters, range and collection bindings, position indices, option and choice captures and inline catches. R1 adds local, parameter and capture matrices for file-visible aliases, traits, forward declarations and Core reservations, plus hidden-name and mutable-update positives. Concrete choice, pending generic-choice and pending optional-field captures have exact rejection sites and distinct checked owners. Focused CLI checks and all 2784 integration target results pass. Block catches and template binders share checked owners without dedicated collision cases. Explicit HIR definitions and updates cover place/value initialisers, caller-result binding freshness and all-results-before-writes multi-bind staging. Re-entered loop binders and body-local aliases have HTML runtime coverage.]
            [: JS / HTML]
            [: New-binding checks cover locals, source-visible values and nominal types, type aliases, traits, builtins, dependency bindings, prelude symbols, namespaces and reserved names. R1 corrects the reviewed alias/trait gap through the existing guard and bound visibility, including forward declarations and Core traits, without another name registry. Hidden original names and namespace members remain unavailable as bare names. Existing mutable parameter and local updates remain assignments, not declarations. Source/import collisions use MOTH-RULE-0038 at the authored name without an invented previous-declaration label. Core trait reservations use MOTH-RULE-0039. Syntax-accepted multi-bind collisions reach semantic validation. Structured control flow owns lexical scope, and statically selected Bool branches retain their authored child scope before HIR. There is no general lexical-block construct or control-flow label.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Control flow]
            [: Supported]
            [$md: Broad branch, loop, break, continue and match coverage, including linked nested-helper returns and loop continuation with exact computed output. @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/tree/main/tests/cases/fixed_numeric_range_runtime (fixed_numeric_range_runtime) covers exact integer and binary-float ranges on HTML and recoverable-failure rejection on HTML-Wasm. Constant and source-order range fixtures cover folding and evaluation order.]
            [: JS / HTML]
            [$md: Exact numeric range domains and I32 indices follow @../loops/#range-loops (Range loops). HTML-JS delivers dynamic zero-step and float no-progress failures through the enclosing failure lane. HTML-Wasm rejects recoverable range failure rather than replacing it with a trap.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Assertions]
            [: Partial]
            [: Broad parser, diagnostics, HIR lowering, backend artefact and integration coverage. Dynamic default-message failures execute on HTML and instantiated HTML-Wasm with distinct Error and trap observations. Targeted final generated-demand coverage retains active message providers and removes literal-true validation-only sidecars and assets.]
            [: Frontend / HIR / JS / HTML / HTML-Wasm]
            [: [$html:<code>assert</code>] is a statement-only language intrinsic. It is always checked, unrecoverable and accepts named/default typed messages. JavaScript and HTML evaluate reachable runtime messages lazily on the failure edge; HTML-Wasm accepts default, fully folded and unreachable literal-true runtime messages but rejects reachable runtime construction. Literal-true messages are fully type/generic/evidence checked and normalized, then replaced with typed [$html:<code>none</code>] before HIR, so no TIR, runtime handoff, generated request or executable fact is published. Message construction cannot escape its evaluation through [$html:<code>!</code>], [$html:<code>?</code>], return, break or continue. Authored propagation locations remain distinct from ordinary call mapping. [$html:<code>assert(false)</code>] is statically terminal.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Functions and calls]
            [: Partial]
            [: Broad declaration, terminality, call validation, named and positional arguments, defaults, parameter passing, external calls and return coverage.]
            [: Frontend / HIR / JS / HTML]
            [: Named calls and defaulted parameters are supported. Source function and generic declarations are module-top-level only. A declaration header inside an executable body is rejected with a structured placement diagnostic. Non-unit success-return functions are validated for all-path terminality before HIR. External and compiler-owned builtin calls remain positional-only. Source-authored return-alias syntax has been removed. HIR signatures, calls and returns preserve ordered success slots separately from an optional error channel. Ordinary return preserves allocation identity, explicit [$html:<code>copy</code>] creates independence and each call result receives a separate caller binding. Separate slots do not prove distinct allocation origins; current multi-return alias analysis keeps conservative root unions, which can produce stricter alias diagnostics.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Default arguments and field defaults]
            [: Supported]
            [$md: Broad function parameter and struct field default coverage, including exact declared types, omission versus explicit optional overrides, named and constructor default skipping and diagnostics. @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/tree/main/tests/cases/numeric_declaration_boundaries_runtime (numeric_declaration_boundaries_runtime) covers a full-range U64 field default.]
            [: Frontend / JS / HTML]
            [: Function parameters and struct fields can define defaults with [$html:<code>=</code>].]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Templates and style directives]
            [: Supported]
            [: Broad template parsing, directive, slot, const/runtime fragment, template control-flow, markdown inline-code and link-separator, heading section-link, diagnostics and JS/HTML integration coverage. Focused literal [$html:<code>$doc</code>] and [$html:<code>$code</code>] preservation, single-arm Bool and option inclusion, lazy loops, structural no-output, empty-string and whitespace slot replay, dependent const-loop headers over enclosing iteration and option-capture bindings, and normal-grammar rejection of removed template markers.]
            [: HTML / JS]
            [: Main Alpha surface. Supported shapes include const and runtime templates, slots/inserts, [$html:<code>$children</code>] / [$html:<code>$fresh</code>] routing, markdown/code/raw/html/css/escape directives, single-arm template Bool and option-present inclusion, template loops, runtime slot applications, dependency namespace member access in template heads and const expansion guards. Template [$html:<code>&#91;else&#93;</code>], [$html:<code>&#91;else if ...&#93;</code>], [$html:<code>&#91;break&#93;</code>] and [$html:<code>&#91;continue&#93;</code>] are removed and rejected by the ordinary grammar. False or absent conditions and zero-output loops suppress dependent wrappers. Structural output remains distinct from emitted empty strings, and slot replay preserves authored whitespace. Static [$html:<code>$md</code>] headings and implicit [$html:<code>$md</code>] in [$html:<code>.mtf</code>] files render automatic fragment IDs from their visible labels, with opaque labels omitting IDs, and plain [$html:<code>.md</code>] files keep their separate renderer with no automatic IDs. Post-TIR performance follow-ups remain accepted deferred implementation tracked below and in the roadmap. [$html:<code>$code("moth")</code>] uses compiler-owned current word classification, lexical token roles and shared code presentation classes, and performs no semantic symbol resolution. Former dollar binding/parameter forms and [$html:<code>$(source)</code>] interpolation receive [$html:<code>MOTH-SYNTAX-0007</code>] at the dollar marker, with lexer and source-case coverage. Recognised template directives remain supported. Presentation-only roles cover final-design [$html:<code>into</code>] placement and canonical [$html:<code>Dec</code>] scale names without reserving them, and a shared error role marks Moth [$html:<code>!</code>] alongside JS/TS [$html:<code>throw</code>]/[$html:<code>catch</code>] and Python [$html:<code>raise</code>]/[$html:<code>except</code>]. Template bodies stay plain authored text around highlighted heads and interpolations.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Moth template [$html:<code>.mtf</code>] content assets]
            [: Partial]
            [: Targeted compiler-integrated dependency binding, synthetic content constant, markdown folding, automatic heading IDs, module-root re-export, direct API source snapshots and cross-document warning contexts, diagnostics and integration coverage. Selected ordinary and module-root single-file entries cover direct Moth template file values, implicit HTML helpers and final source-identity rebinding.]
            [: HTML builder / frontend]
            [: [$html:<code>.mtf</code>] files are builder-supported source assets that synthesize one private compile-time [$html:<code>content #String</code>] constant from an implicit [$html:<code>$md</code>] template body. Static headings inherit [$html:<code>$md</code>] automatic fragment IDs. Nested templates with no explicit directive also default to [$html:<code>$md</code>], and any explicit nested directive overrides that default. They bind extensionlessly into [$html:<code>.moth</code>], can be re-exported through a module root's [$html:<code>export:</code>] block, and must fully fold. Same-directory constants and builder constants remain visible when their names are unique. A collision reports both source locations where available, and neither source shadows the other.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Plain Markdown [$html:<code>.md</code>] content assets]
            [: Supported]
            [: Broad parser, constructor, default, receiver, cross-file, borrow and diagnostics coverage.]
            [: JS / HTML]
            [: Source-authored receiver methods are statically resolved and belong to the same file as their nominal receiver type. External packages do not expose receiver methods.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Choices]
            [: Supported]
            [: Broad declaration, constructor, dependency, return, assignment, match, payload, JS carrier-shape and structural equality coverage. Targeted adversarial traces observe every loop variant, small/large nested-handler outcomes and successful/caught-error choice results.]
            [: JS / HTML]
            [: Unit and record-payload variants are supported. Payload fields are immutable. Accepted incomplete work includes direct payload field access with narrowing, nested payload patterns and recursive choices.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Pattern matching]
            [: Partial]
            [$md: Broad literal, choice, payload-capture, relational, guard, exhaustiveness, warning and value-if coverage. @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/tree/main/tests/cases/match_optional_u8_literal_pattern_success (match_optional_u8_literal_pattern_success) covers exact optional numeric payload patterns. Fixed-width and Byte typing fixtures cover range and category diagnostics.]
            [: Frontend / HIR / JS / HTML]
            [: Literal patterns, choice variants, payload extraction, relational patterns, guards and [$html:<code>else =&gt;</code>] arms work on HTML-JS. Option captures and declared choice payload captures remain valid, while general bare-name captures and String relational subjects receive diagnostics. Closed receivers support inline and block single-predicate value matches. Full relational overlap analysis and nested choice payload patterns remain deferred. Fixed numeric scalar patterns do not grant HTML-Wasm aggregate support.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Collections]
            [: Supported]
            [$md: Broad literal, mutation, indexing, loop, fixed-capacity, failure and diagnostics coverage, including multiline qualified choice constructors retained through header discovery. A deterministic fulfilment ledger checks stock conservation across repeated updates, partial requests, returns and caught helper errors. @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/tree/main/tests/cases/aggregate_late_u64_collection_runtime (aggregate_late_u64_collection_runtime) covers body-local numeric leaf inference. Concrete element mismatch fixtures reject storage widening.]
            [: JS / HTML]
            [$md: Growable [$html:<code>{T}</code>] and fixed [$html:<code>{N T}</code>] collections retain distinct types. Positive literals and visible [$html:<code>#I32</code>] constants supply fixed capacities. Numeric element inference follows @../collections/#collection-literals (Collection literals), while positions and lengths use I32. Growable push remains infallible and returns no value. Fixed push, get, set and remove retain their documented failure contracts. HTML-Wasm rejects collection lowering, including every collection holding a fixed scalar.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Hash maps]
            [: Partial]
            [$md: Broad frontend, HIR, borrow, JS and diagnostic coverage. @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/tree/main/tests/cases/aggregate_late_u64_map_keys_runtime (aggregate_late_u64_map_keys_runtime) and duplicate-key fixtures cover numeric key inference. Float-key fixtures cover target rejection.]
            [: JS / HTML / backend validation for other targets]
            [$md: HTML-JS supports insertion-ordered maps with fixed integer, Byte and canonical String-like keys, exact typed lookup and duplicate rejection. F16/F32/F64 and Dec keys remain unsupported. Numeric leaves follow @../collections/#hash-maps (Hash maps). Current contextual [$html:<code>{}</code>] empty maps differ from the accepted [$html:<code>{=}</code>] spelling, which remains deferred. HTML-Wasm rejects map lowering.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Results, options, multiple returns and multi-bind]
            [: Supported]
            [$md: Broad options, propagation, catches, value blocks, multiple returns, multi-bind and diagnostics coverage. Single-value receivers reject zero or multiple success slots with factual arity diagnostics. @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/tree/main/tests/cases/success_shape_matching_multi_bind_runtime (success_shape_matching_multi_bind_runtime) covers ordered, exactly-once production before target writes. @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/tree/main/tests/cases/grouped_multi_bind_call_group_runtime (grouped_multi_bind_call_group_runtime) covers a parenthesised pending call accepted by a matching multi-bind. @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/tree/main/tests/cases/optional_propagation_contract_runtime (optional_propagation_contract_runtime) covers HTML execution and pins the existing HTML-Wasm mutable-local-binding-alias gate's exact reason and source site. Both target assertions pass in the 0b99f7470 integrated gate. @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/tree/main/tests/cases/catch_fallback_u64_constrains_pending_arithmetic_runtime (catch_fallback_u64_constrains_pending_arithmetic_runtime) covers handler constraints. @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/tree/main/tests/cases/value_catch_unreachable_map_fallback_runtime (value_catch_unreachable_map_fallback_runtime) covers HTML-Wasm handler pruning.]
            [: Frontend / HIR / JS / HTML]
            [$md: HTML-JS delivers typed errors, options, private inferred failure lanes and authored safety catches with expression-wide recovery. HIR uses native ordered result slots and edge-defined success/error destinations rather than semantic tuples or fallible carriers. JavaScript packing stays at ABI boundaries. Postfix propagation keeps the enclosing callable contract in nested branches. Optional [$html:<code>?</code>] rejects multiple success returns with their authored count, separately from a missing optional return. Entry [$html:<code>!</code>] rejects an incompatible custom error separately from a function without an error slot. Handlers contribute constraints and undergo source validation before unreachable handlers disappear. Eligibility and delivery follow @../errors/#catch-and-recovery (Catch and recovery). Value-producing if and match work at declaration, assignment, return and multi-bind receivers. Nested then receivers remain deferred. HTML-Wasm rejects recoverable numeric failure but a pruned handler adds no target requirement.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Constants and compile-time folding]
            [: Supported]
            [$md: Broad constant, dependency sorting, template, numeric, const-record, external constant, config and diagnostic coverage. @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/tree/main/tests/cases/numeric_module_constants_generic_identity_runtime (numeric_module_constants_generic_identity_runtime) covers imported exact identities. Constant inference boundary fixtures reject later retyping.]
            [: Frontend]
            [$md: Constants fully fold and resolve numeric types at their declaration boundary. Later runtime receivers cannot retype them. Numeric folding retains exact types and configuration provenance and diagnoses known failures at the authored operation. Const records remain compile-time field groups, with exported and imported folded records supported. Anonymous const records use named-only entries and permit nested folded records without declaring constructable types. See @../constants (Constants).]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Type aliases]
            [: Partial]
            [: Broad builtin, imported, collection, option, generic instance, alias-to-alias, remap, fixed-capacity constant, trait-requirement and diagnostic coverage.]
            [: Frontend]
            [: Transparent aliases to builtins, source and imported source types, collections, maps, options and concrete generic instances are supported, including targets whose fixed collection capacity folds a module constant. Alias targets keep local nominal identity through generic materialisation. The compiler still rejects aliases to external opaque types despite the accepted transparent-alias contract.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Arithmetic, explicit casts and copy]
            [: Partial]
            [$md: Broad arithmetic, cast, folding, copy and diagnostic coverage. @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/tree/main/tests/cases/numeric_equal_type_arithmetic_matrix (numeric_equal_type_arithmetic_matrix) and @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/tree/main/tests/cases/numeric_mixed_type_arithmetic_matrix (numeric_mixed_type_arithmetic_matrix) cover result domains. Narrow I8/U8 failure and F16 completion fixtures include release parity. @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/tree/main/tests/cases/numeric_semantic_physical_variants_debug (numeric_semantic_physical_variants_debug) covers HTML/HTML-Wasm scalar parity.]
            [: Frontend / HIR / JS / HTML / HTML-Wasm]
            [$md: The exact scalar, Dec, Byte and plain cast contracts are delivered through the frontend and HTML-JS. @../numbers (Numbers) and @../casts (Casts) own defaults, result domains, precision and evidence. Cast failure follows ordinary local recovery, private inference or compatible Error! delivery. Copy retains runtime independence. HTML-Wasm executes supported exact scalars, comparisons, formatting and infallible numeric casts, with conservative integer proofs removing only discharged checks. It rejects recoverable numeric failure and every user aggregate holding a fixed scalar before lowering. Dec values, statement casts, unsupported conversions and foreign calls remain target-gated. F16 computation carriers do not widen its semantic precision. Const Error materialisation and branching const catch handlers remain deferred.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Binary-float formatting and boundary validation]
            [: Partial]
            [$md: Broad folding, formatting and foreign-boundary coverage. @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/tree/main/tests/cases/external_fixed_scalars_int32_float64 (external_fixed_scalars_int32_float64) covers exact F32 annotations, rounding and foreign failure delivery. Backend tests cover all finite F16 formatter encodings, F32/F64 boundary bits, wrong carriers, signed zero, subnormals and non-finite guards.]
            [: JS / HTML / HTML-Wasm]
            [$md: HTML-JS formats exact binary floats and validates foreign results at their declared F16/F32/F64 precision without coercing non-Number carriers. HIR retains the exact type through raw and validated success slots. Integrity guards do not become catch producers without a source-declared Error! contract. HTML-Wasm formats supported F16/F32/F64 scalars and lowers trapping F32/F64 guards, while F16 validation, recoverable guards, foreign calls and String-to-numeric parsing remain target-rejected. Annotated JavaScript signature support belongs to @./packages-and-builders (Packages and Builders).]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Type checking and coercion]
            [: Supported]
            [$md: Broad compatibility, receiver, call, return and diagnostic coverage. @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/tree/main/tests/cases/scalar_late_u64_full_literal_receiver (scalar_late_u64_full_literal_receiver), @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/tree/main/tests/cases/scalar_receiver_conflict_deterministic (scalar_receiver_conflict_deterministic) and generic signature fixtures cover bounded inference and authored-order conflict witnesses.]
            [: Frontend]
            [$md: Body-local numeric inference resolves literal origins through known scalar, field, collection, map and generic signature receivers before I32/F64 defaulting. Concrete boundaries remain exact. Cast targets do not constrain their source and completed named arithmetic does not infer backwards. No pending type crosses a body or module boundary. See @../numbers/#numeric-literals (Numeric literals).]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Traits]
            [: Partial]
            [: Broad declaration, conformance, visibility, generic-bound receiver-call, trait-name rejection, core cast trait metadata covering all eleven fixed numeric targets, incompatibility metadata, public API leak and diagnostics coverage.]
            [: Frontend static semantics]
            [: Static trait declarations, explicit same-file nominal conformances, reusable evidence, trait incompatibility metadata and generic bounds are implemented. Accepted incomplete work covers static non-method requirements, additional compiler-owned builtin conformance families and a broader standard trait taxonomy that remains static.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Strings, coercion and IO/template boundaries]
            [: Partial]
            [: Broad quoted-escape, string-content coercion and IO/template rejection coverage, plus targeted external [$html:<code>StringContent</code>] signature coverage.]
            [: Frontend / JS]
            [: Quoted strings decode only [$html:<code>\\</code>], [$html:<code>\"</code>], [$html:<code>\n</code>], [$html:<code>\r</code>] and [$html:<code>\t</code>]. Invalid escapes are rejected by the tokenizer. Template heads and reusable external string-content signatures accept supported string values. Console helpers such as [$html:<code>io.line(...)</code>] use the same boundary, so complex values must be extracted or interpolated into templates before output. AST rejects source [$html:<code>String + String</code>] and no longer folds compile-time string addition. Runtime equality compares String content across construction origins; HTML-JS compares primitive String map keys by content and HTML-Wasm compares finalized string bytes. Accepted design rejects source string concatenation with [$html:<code>+</code>]; templates remain the canonical concatenation form.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Borrow validation and local last-use analysis]
            [: Partial]
            [: Production and Boracle source tests cover alias conflicts, CFG joins, loops, call effects, update legality, optional transfer and reference/oracle differential cases.]
            [: HIR analysis / all backends]
            [: Production access checking is mandatory and currently uses the alpha checker. The live verified implementation inventory, performance evidence, accepted production decisions, Boracle gaps and next work are maintained in @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/blob/main/docs/roadmap/plans/production-borrow-checker.md (the production borrow checker tracker).]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Result provenance and retention summaries]
            [: Partial]
            [: Targeted local return-alias, unknown-result and fresh-result summary tests.]
            [: HIR analysis / link planning]
            [: Complete per-result, detached-storage, retained-parameter, outlives, cardinality, whole-domain kill and outcome-sensitive retention summaries remain unfinished. The borrow-side implementation detail and active handoff are tracked in @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/blob/main/docs/roadmap/plans/production-borrow-checker.md (the production borrow checker tracker).]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Lifetime-region and escape validation]
            [: Deferred]
            [: None]
            [: Backend-neutral design]
            [: Accepted architecture exists; no implementation owner has landed. This validation is mandatory in the accepted design, and no backend or profile may bypass it. Complete when mandatory local and link topology proof is implemented.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Retained-edge liveness and cleanup frontiers]
            [: Deferred]
            [: None]
            [: Backend-neutral design]
            [: Accepted design only. Covers retention domains, edge creation, whole-domain kill effects and path-sensitive final cleanup frontiers. Builtin collections do not yet expose [$html:<code>clear</code>] or compiler-known retention effects outside maps. Complete when whole-domain effects, frontiers and epochs are implemented.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Compiler-generated intervals and region epochs]
            [: Deferred]
            [: None]
            [: Backend-neutral design]
            [: Accepted design only. Inferred regions must be non-lexical CFG intervals rather than lexical scopes, with repeated population and teardown forming region epochs. Complete when non-lexical interval inference and epochs are implemented.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Declared regions (`name:` / `into name`)]
            [: Deferred]
            [: Targeted lexer, statement-classification, deferred-diagnostic, anonymous-declared-region rejection and reclaimed-identifier coverage.]
            [: Frontend / HIR / lifetime analysis / all backends]
            [: Valid [$html:<code>name:</code>] headers are reserved in executable statement position and report a deferred-feature diagnostic. Exact [$html:<code>_:</code>] is rejected. Full declared-region parsing, [$html:<code>into name</code>] placement, HIR metadata, topology and backend support remain deferred. Declared regions are hard count-free bulk lifetimes whose owned storage is never released individually.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Direct source-created reference cycles]
            [: Deferred]
            [: None]
            [: Frontend / lifetime analysis / all backends]
            [: Accepted design only. A declared region is the only source mechanism for reference cycles, and inferred-region analysis never invents a cyclic region. Complete when direct source-created cycle construction and bulk cleanup are implemented.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Affine Ownership ABI]
            [: Experimental]
            [: Targeted Wasm ownership-projection and conditional-drop coverage.]
            [: Wasm]
            [: Present but not part of the Alpha stability promise. Borrow-checker advisory drop sites currently emit Wasm [$html:<code>DropIfOwned</code>] directly under current scaffolding, and the LIR carries a reserved retain instruction. This direct path is migration debt, not accepted final architecture. Complete when memory-plan-driven full-control lowering replaces it.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Memory-strategy planning]
            [: Deferred]
            [: None]
            [: Backend-neutral design]
            [: Accepted design only. A distinct compiler-owned planner must select stack, inline, affine, inferred-region, declared-region, REC or host garbage-collected representation per allocation family after topology is proven, and must also produce the concrete per-family obligation transitions the backend encodes. A missing strategy after successful validation is a [$html:<code>CompilerError</code>] in the accepted design. No [$html:<code>ValidatedMemoryPlan</code>] implementation owner exists yet. Complete when the planner owns every strategy decision and its plan is validated before lowering.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: REC analysis and selection]
            [: Deferred]
            [: None]
            [: Backend-neutral design]
            [: Accepted design only. Retained Edge Counting is a compiler-selected physical representation for runtime-many persistent retained edges, never source semantics. The canonical contract lives under `docs/src/developer-docs/memory-management/retained-edge-counting/`. Complete when the companion analysis and its decision reporting are implemented.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: REC backend lowering]
            [: Deferred]
            [: None]
            [: Full-control backends]
            [: Accepted design only. Covers the two-bit tagged handle ABI, selective inline counters, count transitions and iterative destruction. Complete when tags, counters, collections and iterative destruction are implemented.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Field-sensitive allocation splitting]
            [: Deferred]
            [: None]
            [: Backend-neutral design]
            [: Accepted design only. Projections currently remain rooted in their containing allocation family. Complete when split analysis and strategy rerun are implemented.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Inferred-region and declared-region backend lowering]
            [: Deferred]
            [: None]
            [: Full-control backends]
            [: Accepted design only. Wasm runtime memory currently provides basic linear-memory page and heap-base planning with no allocator, inferred-region, declared-region or destruction-plan support. Complete when inferred-region allocation with complete exits and declared-region bulk cleanup are implemented.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Physical coalescing]
            [: Deferred]
            [: None]
            [: Backend-neutral planning / full-control backends]
            [: Accepted optional optimisation only. Physical coalescing may keep storage slightly longer than the maximally precise semantic interval, but it never widens semantic topology or changes diagnostics. Complete when the narrow straight-line heuristic is implemented and verified.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Builder lifecycle regions]
            [: Deferred]
            [: None]
            [: Project/link planning / full-control backends]
            [: Accepted design only. Page, mount, request, frame and arena roots must participate in ordinary lifetime-topology validation for retained builder-owned storage and future observation or event lifecycles. Complete when builder lifecycle roots instantiate validated topology and memory plans.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Collector-free release verification]
            [: Deferred]
            [: None]
            [: Full-control backends / project builders]
            [: Accepted design only. Backends must declare collector-free release capability, and a capable backend must not fall back to a tracing collector. This is a backend capability and an artefact property, never a source-visible or project-visible mode. Complete when a capable backend proves no tracing runtime remains.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Channels]
            [: Deferred]
            [: None]
            [: Async / lifetime planning / full-control backends]
            [: Accepted prerequisites are mandatory affine transfer on successful send, responsibility preservation on failed send, bounded channel or task lifetimes, independent graphs across task boundaries and non-atomic REC under the cooperative task model. The future form `reader, writer Channel of AppMessage = Channel()` has directional endpoints but no compiler or runtime implementation; omitted initialisation is unsupported. Complete when the final memory prerequisites and a separate channel implementation plan are accepted.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Wiring parameter contracts]
            [: Deferred]
            [: None]
            [: JS / HTML after Phase 6]
            [$md: Wire and Route are accepted parameter contracts, but the current Alpha compiler does not support their source syntax. Phase 6 owns implementation; this does not include route application or composition, subscriptions, event delivery, channels or async. See @../wiring (Wiring) for the canonical contract.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Debug and GC-native representation parity]
            [: Partial]
            [: Broad JS pipeline coverage; no cross-backend memory parity suite.]
            [: JS / host GC]
            [: The JS backend uses host collection for already-legal topology, which is accepted behaviour for a GC-native target. Accepted incomplete work: parity currently rests on borrow validation alone because lifetime-topology validation is deferred, so the shared legality guarantee is not yet fully enforced. Complete when debug, GC-native and collector-free paths demonstrably share source legality and observable behaviour.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: WIT value-only component integration]
            [: Deferred]
            [: None]
            [: Frontend / build / backends]
            [: Accepted closed external-boundary profile; no current import validator or runtime integration.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Paths and dependency clauses]
            [: Supported]
            [: Broad source dependency, namespace, flat direct-selection, binding-backed package, public-surface, module-boundary, collision, continuation-diagnostic and legacy-import-migration coverage.]
            [: Frontend / HTML builder]
            [: Source and module namespace records are shallow. External package namespace records support nested symbol paths such as [$html:<code>io.input.new</code>] and [$html:<code>io.input.Input</code>]. Direct symbol-path dependencies remain rejected. Cross-module and source-backed package access goes through the target root's [$html:<code>export:</code>] public surface.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: File paths in value position]
            [: Supported]
            [: Broad prepared-reference and Stage 0 physical-input resolution, fixed-point content discovery, AST String/file-kind semantics, structural Resource/SiteRoot flow, public/generated/service boundaries, provider JS identity, output-link planning, boundary, symlink, missing-watch, config-rejection and ordinary user-defined [$html:<code>Path</code>] coverage.]
            [: Frontend / HTML builder]
            [: Every physical-target-bearing non-dependency path token is classified during retained preparation and resolved once during Stage 0. Referenced [$html:<code>.mtf</code>] and [$html:<code>.md</code>] sources extend preparation to a fixed point; ordinary resources produce byte-free physical input and watch records; [$html:<code>SourceKindNoFileValue</code>] for [$html:<code>.moth</code>] plus extensionless and site-root rows remain no-physical-target outcomes for AST. AST interprets published outcomes as ordinary [$html:<code>String</code>] values, including structural Resource/SiteRoot pieces. Direct [$html:<code>.mtf</code>] compilation consumes the same Stage 0 facts, provider JS assets share resource identity and IO authority and CSS validation preserves opaque anchors. Config bootstrap rejects file-value paths. The superseded source-visible [$html:<code>Path</code>] type is gone, so [$html:<code>Path</code>] is an ordinary user identifier.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Logical expressions]
            [: Supported]
            [: Broad precedence, grouping, invalid operand, not, comparison, short-circuit and JS operator mapping coverage.]
            [: JS / HTML]
            [: Keep short-circuit behavior covered through artifact-shaped tests.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: If statements and conditions]
            [: Supported]
            [: Broad condition validation, nested condition, Bool requirement, same-line else-if rejection and JS lowering coverage.]
            [: JS / HTML]
            [: Conditions require Bool unless the specific syntax is an option or match-style selector.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Char]
            [: Supported]
            [: Broad success coverage with targeted diagnostic coverage.]
            [: JS / HTML]
            [: Additional malformed-char diagnostics are cleanup, not a core Alpha blocker.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Named arguments and call-site mutability]
            [: Partial]
            [: Broad function-call, constructor-call, duplicate, unknown, missing, ordering and tilde diagnostics coverage.]
            [: HTML-JS / HTML-Wasm]
            [: Fresh rvalues can satisfy mutable parameters without [$html:<code>~</code>]. Existing places still require explicit [$html:<code>~</code>]. HTML-JS preserves normal write-through; HTML-Wasm rejects reachable mutable function parameters because its call-argument ABI has no writable argument slot.]
        ]
    ]
]

#[section, $md:
    ## Project and build-system model

    [styled_table:
        [header: [: Surface] [: Status] [: Coverage] [: Runtime target] [: Notes] ]

        [data:
            [: Project config model]
            [: Supported]
            [$md: Targeted grouped-record, builder-schema, input, static-specialisation, provenance and boundary coverage. @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/tree/main/tests/cases/config_contract_u32_success (config_contract_u32_success) covers a source U32 contract. @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/blob/main/src/compiler_frontend/tests/build_config_tests.rs (build config tests) covers receiving-width materialisation, deferred range failures and direct F16/F32 midpoint rounding.]
            [: Frontend / build system]
            [: Grouped project and active builder records remain the implemented config surface. Declaration-owned [$html:<code>#Config of T</code>] inputs accept every fixed integer and binary-float contract. Command numbers retain literal spelling until the exact receiving contract materialises them. Typed inputs preserve their exact identity. Build, check and dev share input handling, immutable [$html:<code>@project</code>] fields, provenance and semantic fingerprints. Ordinary static if consumes folded Bool values before HIR. The queued directive design remains separately deferred below.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: General non-template directives]
            [: Deferred]
            [: None]
            [: Frontend]
            [$md: Accepted queued design. [$html:<code>$</code>] is the general marker, sharing call syntax with ordinary calls except that authored empty [$html:<code>()</code>] is invalid. Template directives remain implemented. See @../directives (Directives).]
        ]

        [data:
            [: [$html:<code>$config</code>] contracts]
            [: Deferred]
            [: None]
            [: Frontend / build system]
            [: Accepted queued design. Prefix on one typed top-level [$html:<code>#</code>] binding. Explicit-only providers; project fields never implicitly supply inputs. Current [$html:<code>#Config</code>] remains the implemented spelling.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: [$html:<code>$project</code>] configuration]
            [: Deferred]
            [: None]
            [: Frontend / build system]
            [$md: Accepted queued design. Closed six-parameter signature with required String [$html:<code>name</code>] and required String [$html:<code>version</code>]. No default version, arbitrary fields or [$html:<code>metadata</code>] argument. HTML [$html:<code>$html_builder</code>] status lives in @./packages-and-builders (Packages and Builders).]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Structural [$html:<code>$feature</code>] selection]
            [: Deferred]
            [: None]
            [: Frontend / build system]
            [: Accepted direction, not ordinary static [$html:<code>if</code>]. Pre-graph source selection and config-only feature declarations wait for their TODO plan. Multi-builder projects depend on this.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Struct layout directives]
            [: Deferred]
            [: None]
            [: Frontend / target validation]
            [: Accepted direction for compiler-owned [$html:<code>$layout</code>] representation constraints. Nominal identity stays unchanged. Not implemented and not a C ABI or SoA commitment.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: [$html:<code>$export</code>] migration]
            [: Deferred]
            [: None]
            [: Frontend]
            [: Implemented visibility remains [$html:<code>export:</code>]. Future compiler-owned [$html:<code>$export</code>] will replace it. Block support is undecided.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Alternative root purposes]
            [: Deferred]
            [: None]
            [: Frontend / build system]
            [: [$html:<code>$test</code>] is a later non-page purpose after currently queued work. No runner, command or lifecycle is defined. HTML [$html:<code>$page</code>] status is tracked in the Packages and Builders Progress Matrix.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Directive block spellings]
            [: Deferred]
            [: None]
            [: Frontend]
            [: Future compiler-owned blocks use [$html:<code>$async:</code>] and [$html:<code>$checked:</code>]. Recognition of those names is not semantic implementation. Checked deep-proof algorithms remain an open proposal, not accepted Alpha work.]
        ]
    ]
]

#[section, $md:
    ## Compiler-owned builtin surfaces

    [styled_table:
        [header: [: Surface] [: Status] [: Coverage] [: Runtime target] [: Notes] ]

        [data:
            [: Collection methods]
            [: Supported]
            [: Broad JS helper, split growable and fixed push helpers, fallible get/set/remove and fixed push, infallible growable push and length, growable handling rejection, fixed overflow, removed indexed-write, bounds and mutable-receiver coverage.]
            [: JS / HTML]
            [: Collection get, set, push, remove and length are compiler-owned members. Mutating calls, including both push forms, require explicit mutable receiver access. One source spelling [$html:<code>push(value)</code>] resolves from the receiver's collection shape: growable push is infallible and rejects [$html:<code>!</code>] or [$html:<code>catch</code>] handling, while fixed push is fallible only when the collection is full and requires [$html:<code>!</code>] or [$html:<code>catch</code>]. The JS runtime exposes separate growable and fixed helpers; HTML-Wasm collection lowering remains deferred.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Receiver methods]
            [: Supported]
            [: Broad same-file struct/choice receiver, aligned generic receiver, imported generic receiver, namespace receiver type, module-boundary exposure, nonlocal/builtin/external receiver rejection, duplicate, collision and mutability coverage. Mutable self calls and nested collection-field mutation execute through reserved receiver roots. Invalid mutable operands retain the offending token's exact span.]
            [: JS / HTML]
            [: Source-authored receiver methods travel with receiver type visibility, including retained generic receiver templates across module and package boundaries. They are never selected independently, aliased or exposed as namespace-record fields.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Fallible suffix handling]
            [: Supported]
            [: Broad propagation, shared then catch recovery, catch handler, template-boundary, explicit HIR Invoke, direct-return, call-argument, receiver-base, match-guard and obsolete HIR propagation-expression removal coverage.]
            [: Frontend / HIR / JS / HTML]
            [: Fallible calls and operations lower through an Invoke terminator with mutually exclusive success/error edges: ordered success destinations are defined only on success and one disjoint error destination only on failure. Value-producing catch recovery joins individual slots through ordinary CFG targets. Propagation transfers the error channel without tuple or payload extraction. Collection and map fallible members and resolved fallible cast conversions use the same selected failure context.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Builtin Error type]
            [: Supported]
            [$md: Broad constructor, default, public-field, source/Core/foreign recovery and range-rejection coverage in @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/tree/main/tests/cases/error_code_u32_runtime (error_code_u32_runtime) and companion error_code_u32 fixtures. @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/blob/main/crates/moth-mon/tests/public_api/numeric.rs (MON public numeric tests) covers U32-schema round trips.]
            [: Frontend / JS]
            [: Public fields use [$html:<code>message String</code>] and [$html:<code>code U32 = 0</code>]. HTML-JS preserves the full unsigned code through construction, propagation, recovery and foreign projection. HTML-Wasm rejects reachable Error values. Hidden location/trace metadata remains backend-internal.]
        ]
    ]
]

#[section, $md:
    ## Experimental backend surfaces

    [styled_table:
        [header: [: Surface] [: Status] [: Current role] [: Alpha rule] ]

        [data:
            [: HTML-Wasm]
            [: Experimental]
            [$md: The experimental backend executes selected scalar and page-fragment paths through the shared HTML builder slot/hydration contract. @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/tree/main/tests/cases/numeric_semantic_physical_variants_debug (numeric_semantic_physical_variants_debug) records current scalar parity, not future aggregate support.]
            [: Keep behind [$html:<code>--html-wasm</code>]. Tests may pin current behavior, but Alpha support is JS / HTML.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Wasm backend]
            [: Experimental]
            [: Long-term backend direction with current HIR-to-Wasm-LIR lowering, runtime contracts, request validation, debug output and binary emission work.]
            [: Do not count Wasm support as an Alpha blocker except for clean diagnostics and no-panic guarantees on covered paths.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Wasm payload/runtime parity]
            [: Experimental]
            [$md: @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/tree/main/tests/cases/numeric_semantic_physical_variants_debug (numeric_semantic_physical_variants_debug) and its release companion cover exact scalar parity. @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/tree/main/tests/cases/fixed_width_aggregate_byte_runtime (fixed_width_aggregate_byte_runtime) covers HTML-JS execution and HTML-Wasm aggregate rejection. @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/tree/main/tests/cases/native_four_mixed_results_runtime (native_four_mixed_results_runtime) passes four native ordered I32/F32/I64/F64 results on HTML and HTML-Wasm. Internal real lowering and emitted Node observer tests own ABI types, permuted destinations and one producer invocation. The F32 value 1.25 does not prove rounding, while 9007199254740993 exposes I64 precision. Fallible Invoke and error-typed signatures remain target-gated. Recoverable numeric, compound write-back and range failures receive capability diagnostics before lowering rather than trap fallbacks. Supported scalar paths include exact comparisons, unsigned boundary formatting, selected infallible casts and F16 rounding. Emitter-only compact-storage tests do not establish source aggregate layout. Runtime Bool interpolation remains a separate gap. Reachable mutable parameters and local binding aliases, optional alias/variant paths, Error values, Dec, foreign calls and unsupported casts remain target-gated. See mutable_local_alias_wasm_rejected and mutable_local_self_update_wasm_supported for the alias distinction.]
            [: Backend parity progress belongs here, not repeated in every feature row.]
        ]
    ]
]

#[section, $md:
    ## Compiler implementation and tooling

    [styled_table:
        [header: [: Surface] [: Status] [: Coverage] [: Current status] ]

        [data:
            [: MON standalone Rust codec]
            [: Partial]
            [$md: Broad public API, codec and bounded hardening coverage. @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/blob/main/crates/moth-mon/tests/public_api/numeric.rs (MON public numeric tests) covers all eleven exact scalar identities, Byte, F16 edge bits, signed zero, U32 Error fields and retained Integer/Decimal values. @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/tree/main/src/compiler_tests/mon_syntax_parity (MON/source syntax parity tests) distinguishes shared syntax from data-only capabilities.]
            [$md: The standalone moth-mon crate and moth::mon re-export expose one Rust-only codec with explicit schemas, exact-width values, immutable prepared defaults, strict field/choice/map routing and bounded failure handling. Integer and Decimal remain lossless data families. Fixed float ingress rounds directly at its declared precision. @../mon (MON) owns the schema and data contracts. Shared lexical rules retain reserved-name and entry-spacing validation, with [$html:<code>{=}</code>] as the MON empty-map spelling. This codec supplies no Moth source/runtime operations or static asset builder.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Moth-native MON integration]
            [: Deferred]
            [: None]
            [: Separate accepted follow-up for compiler-owned [$html:<code>$mon</code>] convenience, schemas derived from ordinary Moth types, checked anonymous-record generation, source/runtime encode/decode operations and the static [$html:<code>.mon</code>] asset builder. Exact directive and command syntax remains undecided. Unicode escapes and contextual [$html:<code>::Variant</code>] construction remain undelivered despite their availability in MON data.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Frontend Arena + Semantic Invariant Optimisation]
            [: Partial]
            [: Broad benchmark, profiling, adversarial-fixture, scope-frame, capacity-estimate and historical validation coverage. Focused typed-store tests cover checked extents, owner isolation, compaction, detached generic preparation and owned service/diagnostic lifetimes.]
            [: Implemented pieces include token/header statistics, conservative capacity estimates, scope-frame arenas and seeding, external registry clone reduction, dense HIR and phase-owned typed expression stores. Phase 4 completes the typed-store, result and lifetime cutover. Repeated type/default/fold work is being reduced by the active Phase 5, while broader template/render-plan, bump-allocation and persistent-cache work remains deferred.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Static Bool [$html:<code>if</code>] specialisation]
            [: Implemented]
            [: Frontend pipeline, HIR boundary, terminality, branch scope, inactive diagnostics and generated-request coverage.]
            [: Both branches remain frontend-valid. A known Bool selects one compiler-generated lexical scope before terminality, durable generated work and HIR. Runtime conditions retain ordinary branch lowering, and source structure never depends on the condition.]
        ]
    ]
]

#[section, $md:
    ## Accepted deferred and incomplete surfaces

    [styled_table:
        [header: [: Surface] [: Status] [: Coverage / diagnostic direction] [: Notes] ]

        [data:
            [: Expression-wide catch and private implicit propagation]
            [: Partial]
            [$md: Broad JS/HTML private propagation, arithmetic, cast, compound write-back, source-order and handler-failure coverage. @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/tree/main/tests/cases/implicit_failure_nested_argument_call_catch_rejected (implicit_failure_nested_argument_call_catch_rejected) pins the call-count boundary. @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/tree/main/tests/cases/catch_unreachable_handler_invalid_cast_rejected (catch_unreachable_handler_invalid_cast_rejected) pins source validation. @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/tree/main/tests/cases/value_catch_unreachable_map_fallback_runtime (value_catch_unreachable_map_fallback_runtime) pins HTML-Wasm pruning.]
            [$md: HTML-JS delivers private no-slot failure propagation, builtin Error! materialisation and expression-wide recovery. @../errors/#catch-and-recovery (Catch and recovery) owns authored safety eligibility, call-count levels, compatible error types and placement. Handlers contribute constraints and undergo full source validation, then disappear only after relevant failure summaries converge. Pruned handlers publish no executable HIR, generated request or backend requirement. Handler failure goes outward. Fatal assertions and integrity guards remain unrecoverable. HTML-Wasm rejects recoverable numeric failure rather than using trap fallback.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Export and custom-error discharge diagnostics]
            [: Partial]
            [: Structured boundary coverage includes direct and multi-hop failure, bounded chains, custom error slots, receiver methods, re-exports and package facades. Self- and mutually recursive helpers whose recursive call precedes the overflow keep the actual operation and cause code at concrete and dormant generic boundaries, and origin-free recursion stays infallible. A later generated request reuses the converged summary; escaping templates are rejected at the declaring dormant boundary before any request materialises.]
            [: Exported no-slot and custom [$html:<code>E!</code>] boundaries reject escaping implicit failure before publication. The diagnostic labels the boundary, at most three private call hops and the originating operation, with typed builtin cause codes and an elided-hop count. Deterministic witnesses skip recursive back-edges while searching for an available origin, preserve donor source ownership and never treat a missing summary as infallible. A cross-module/external leaf stops the witness without inventing an origin. Repair by adding builtin [$html:<code>Error!</code>] or recovering/mapping locally. Deliberate assertion remains an explicit choice. A compound write-back witness names the target type rather than the handled right-hand side.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Start Error! and success-only HTML fragment publication]
            [: Partial]
            [: JS/HTML cases cover implicit and propagated entry failure, code-zero failure, local recovery, ordered once-only success and suppression of all invocation-owned runtime fragments after failure. The execution harness records failure at the generated start call.]
            [: Applicable synthetic [$html:<code>start</code>] has builtin [$html:<code>Error!</code>]. Compatible postfix [$html:<code>!</code>] works at the entry body root and in nested branches. Incompatible custom errors require explicit recovery. HTML publishes runtime fragments only after success, preserves static HTML and earlier explicit IO, and shows fixed safe release text instead of application Error HTML after failure. The terminal reports a fixed notice and unsuccessful execution independently of the error code. See Dev entry-error reporting for the [$html:<code>moth dev</code>] view.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Dev entry-error reporting]
            [: Supported]
            [: Dev-server and generated-bootstrap tests cover returned Errors including code 0, assertion and unexpected startup-fault categories for JS and HTML-Wasm, hostile text, stale, duplicate, malformed, oversized and cross-origin reports, origin prefixes, unavailable transport and isolated pages. A release case proves output has no dev reporting dependency. Barrier tests cover output publication against concurrent requests, companion requests spanning a rebuild and write failure. A real rebuild/write/serve sequence over one output directory proves current output freshness through private-body entry-outcome changes, an invalidated success-only export and unchanged writer skips. Cache-reuse invalidation remains untested because no semantic or physical artefact cache exists yet.]
            [: In [$html:<code>moth dev</code>], a returned [$html:<code>start</code>] Error covers its page with a runtime view that keeps hot reload, logs to the console and reports once to the server terminal. A successful build stays successful for every page. Assertion failures are identified structurally and other faults escaping the generated startup wrapper are reported as unexpected startup faults. Later callbacks and unrelated scripts are not intercepted. Each served response comes from one completed output generation; a page whose companions load after a rebuild has its reports rejected as stale and reloads normally.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Post-TIR template performance follow-ups]
            [: Deferred]
            [: Not part of the current Alpha surface.]
            [: Deferred items are source-span-backed template body text, a per-template parse cache, a formatter-output cache, dev-mode source-hash keyed template reuse, dependency-aware invalidation for imported consts/directives, formatter algorithm rewrites if profiling justifies them, incremental module/template compilation after module-boundary incremental builds exist and parallel nested-template folding after a separate profiling-backed plan.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: [$html:<code>Range</code>] values outside loop headers]
            [: Deferred]
            [: Loop-header ranges are covered with the loop surface. One targeted case pins that a folded [$html:<code>Range</code>] constant survives to HIR; the operations a range would need are not covered because they do not exist yet.]
            [: [$html:<code>start to end</code>] is a loop-header form today. A [$html:<code>Range</code>] can be bound, including as a [$html:<code>#</code>] constant, which folds and lowers, but the bound value is inert: it is not a collection-loop source, exposes no [$html:<code>start</code>] / [$html:<code>end</code>] members and is not renderable in a template head. First-class range values are intended but not designed or implemented, so do not add coverage for range operations until the surface is shaped.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Choice payload field access]
            [: Deferred]
            [: Targeted direct-field-access diagnostic coverage.]
            [: Use pattern matching to extract payload fields until variant narrowing/refinement is designed.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Choice nested payload patterns and richer exhaustiveness]
            [: Deferred]
            [: Targeted nested-pattern deferred diagnostics.]
            [: Exhaustiveness is tag-level for now. General pattern binding renames are also deferred outside choice payload capture renames.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Choice recursive types]
            [: Deferred]
            [: Targeted recursive declaration deferred diagnostics.]
            [: Requires layout and indirection design.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: `as` keyword domains]
            [: Supported]
            [: Targeted success and invalid-position diagnostics.]
            [: [$html:<code>as</code>] is valid for type aliases, dependency namespace/direct-selection aliases and choice payload capture renames. Other positions are rejected.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Module-root export/re-export syntax]
            [: Supported]
            [: Targeted authored export, flat direct-selection re-export, alias, source/external symbol, privacy, duplicate, placement and strict-block rejection coverage. Pure one/two-hop Moth facades retain external Call/Invoke runtime demand without callable Moth wrappers or dormant-root activation.]
            [: A module root's single strict [$html:<code>export:</code>] block exposes public authored declarations and flat dependency re-exports. Legacy inline [$html:<code>export</code>] forms, bare namespace exports, wildcard exports, function alias values and automatic method re-export through type aliases are rejected.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Generic type infrastructure]
            [: Supported]
            [: Broad TypeId, nominal instance, alias, constructor, dependency, HIR validation, trait-bound and diagnostic coverage. Generic applications preserve following parameter and field separators, including shared and mutable receivers and multi-argument applications.]
            [: Generic structs, choices, concrete aliases, generic free functions, aligned generic receiver methods and trait-bound validation use canonical [$html:<code>TypeId</code>] identity before HIR.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Generic functions]
            [: Partial]
            [: Broad declaration, body validation, inference, instantiation, module-boundary dependency, frozen provider semantic-closure defaults and namespaces, nested private receiver materialisation with forwarded receiver bounds and evidence, diagnostics, fallible return and non-value-use coverage. Targeted numeric-origin cases cover later receivers, exact and partly solved result envelopes, mutable scalar targets, typed errors, inactive handlers and inference exclusions.]
            [: Generic free functions and aligned receiver templates infer ordinary types from immediate evidence. Declared parameter/result relationships can carry existing numeric origins to eligible later body-local receivers. Known result leaves retain their exact type while unresolved leaves await completion. Bounds and cast evidence validate after type selection, and only concrete requests reach materialisation. This adds no general later-result inference, callee-body search or outer-parameter forwarding into nested calls. Explicit call-site type application remains outside the inference-only rule.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Trait bounds on generics]
            [: Partial]
            [: Broad declaration-site bound, visible evidence, module public-surface privacy, receiver-call, nominal generic instance and negative diagnostic coverage.]
            [: Bounds use [$html:<code>is</code>]. Concrete generic calls and nominal generic instances require visible reusable evidence. Core cast bounds derive pair-scoped evidence on demand with exact fallibility. Retained requests freeze canonical identities without mutating the frozen requester.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Recursive generic types]
            [: Deferred]
            [: Targeted recursive generic type diagnostics.]
            [: Requires layout and indirection design.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Nested generic type application]
            [: Deferred]
            [: Targeted nested [$html:<code>of</code>] diagnostics and alias workaround coverage.]
            [: Name intermediate concrete aliases instead of writing nested inline generic applications.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Exact numeric types and bounded inference]
            [: Supported]
            [$md: Broad scalar, aggregate and generic source coverage. @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/tree/main/tests/cases/scalar_receiver_conflict_deterministic (scalar_receiver_conflict_deterministic) and its reversed witness fixture cover diagnostics. @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/tree/main/tests/cases/generic_numeric_signature_late_receivers_runtime (generic_numeric_signature_late_receivers_runtime) and @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/tree/main/tests/cases/generic_constructed_numeric_retention_runtime (generic_constructed_numeric_retention_runtime) cover signature and direct-constructor constraints. Arithmetic matrices and release companions cover exact result domains and operation completion.]
            [$md: The frontend and HTML-JS deliver the accepted @../numbers (exact numeric contracts), including bounded body-local inference, declaration-owned constants, known collection/map leaves and generic signature/direct-constructor receivers. Empty containers and non-numeric unknowns gain no general later-use inference. Config, MON and Core packages use exact contracts, with their separate rows recording coverage. HTML-Wasm support remains limited to its recorded scalar paths and explicit gates.]
        ]
    ]
]

#[section, $md:
    ## Maintenance rule

    This matrix should stay concise and current.

    - Move package and project-builder status to @./packages-and-builders (Packages and Builders Progress).
    - Move workflow and benchmark details to `CONTRIBUTING.md`.
    - Move implementation sequencing, active project plans and proposals that have
      not been accepted as design to `docs/roadmap/roadmap.md`.
    - Move accepted end-state architecture to the canonical compiler, memory or
      language design reference, even when implementation is incomplete.
    - Move syntax examples and beginner explanations to docs pages under `docs/src/docs/**`.
    - Move compiler architecture details to `docs/compiler-design-overview.md`.
    - Keep this page focused on core language/compiler support, partial implementation, target gating, experimental backends and accepted deferred implementation. Put open design questions and outside-scope features in @../design-scope (Design scope).
]
